  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDry weather will continue through Wednesday morning as high\npressure maintains influence over the region. The high will\ndrift east on Wednesday, resulting in the return of chances of \nscattered diurnally-driven showers and thunderstorms, primarily\neast of I-71. On Thursday, an upper trough and slow-moving\nfrontal boundary will move east towards the area. While there's\npotential that most of the area could be between more widespread\nshower/thunderstorms to the east and west, scattered showers and\nthunderstorms are possible across the entire area with the \nhighest PoPs across NW OH and the far eastern fringe of the \nlocal area. Locally heavy rainfall will be the main concern with\nthunderstorms Thursday given dew points well into the 60s and \nPWAT values of 1.5 to possibly even 2 inches. The severe weather\nrisk is low given the lack of forcing/shear. \n\nConfidence in widespread showers/storms is highest on Friday as\nthe frontal boundary moves east into the area with scattered \nshowers and storms possible into the weekend as the front slowly\nmoves east across the region. Organized thunderstorms (some\npossibly strong) can't be ruled out since shear will be higher,\nbut will need to see how much we destabilize before peak \ndiurnal instability. It's possible that early day showers and \nstorms limit the severe weather threat. Will need to monitor \nFriday for heavy rainfall due to mean flow parallel to the \nfrontal boundary and continued moisture across the region.\n\nA break in showers/storms is possible at some point over the\nweekend, but it looks like the unsettled weather pattern will\ncontinue late weekend into early next week as zonal flow\ndevelops over the region and disturbances ride east across the \narea.",
